 built. It was surrounded on all sides, except the north, by deep valleys, that of the Tyropoeon separating it from Moriah, which it surpasses in height by 105 feet.","verses":140,"v":[2,5]},{"id":"babylon_151","name":"Babylon","kind":"City","lat":32.53617,"lon":44.42082,"summary":"The Greek form of BABEL; Semitic form Babilu, meaning “The Gate of God.” In the Assyrian tablets it means “The city of the dispersion of the tribes.”","verses":255,"v":[4]},{"id":"cush_312","name":"Cush","kind":"Region","lat":21.95979,"lon":31.34356,"summary":"A son, probably the eldest, of Ham, and the father of Nimrod (Gen. 10:8; 1 Chr. 1:10). From him the land of Cush seems to have derived its name.","verses":16,"v":[4]},{"id":"philistia_943","name":"Philistia","kind":"Region","lat":33.56317,"lon":35.36635,"summary":"Palestine, “the land of the Philistines” (Ps. 60:8; 87:4; 108:9).","verses":6,"v":[4]},{"id":"rahab_969","name":"Rahab","kind":"","lat":30.10809,"lon":31.33822,"summary":"Insolence; pride, a poetical name applied to Egypt in Ps. 87:4; 89:10; Isa. 51:9, as “the proud one.”","verses":3,"v":[4]},{"id":"tyre_1184","name":"Tyre","kind":"City","lat":33.26807,"lon":35.20936,"summary":"A rock, now es-Sur; an ancient Phoenician city, about 23 miles, in a direct line, north of Acre, and 20 south of Sidon. Sidon was the oldest Phoenician city, but Tyre had a longer and more illustrious history.","verses":52,"v":[4]}],"events":[],"traditions":[],"sources":["JFB","MHCC","TDavid","Wesley"],"tags":{"bsb":[[4210,4210,1121,1121,1121,7141,7141,7892,7892,3248,3248,3248,3248,3248,6944,6944,6944,2042],[3068,3068,157,8179,8179,6726,6726,3605,3605,3605,4908,4908,3290,3290],[3513,3513,1696,1696,0,0,5892,5892,430,430,5542],[2142,2142,2142,7293,894,894,3045,3045,3045,3045,3045,2009,6429,6865,5973,3568,2088,2088,2088,2088,3205,3205,8033,8033],[559,559,559,559,559,6726,6726,376,376,376,376,376,3205,3205,0,0,5945,5945,5945,5945,1931,3559,3559,3559],[3068,3068,5608,5608,3789,3789,3789,5971,5971,5971,20